<BlogPost title="Cue Agents vs DeepSeek Harness: personal identity agents meet an open harness | dshbase" description="Manus launched Cue Agents on Sep 28 with email, phone, wallet and computer per agent. Here is how that product maps—and does not map—onto DeepSeek Harness for DSH readers." currentPath="/blog/cue-agents-vs-dsh/" category="Analysis">
  <h1>Cue Agents vs DeepSeek Harness: <span class="accent">personal identity agents meet an open harness</span></h1>
  <p class="muted small" style="margin-bottom:20px">September 29, 2026 · dshbase · X pulse</p>

  <p>On September 28, 2026, Manus shipped Manus 2.0 and, beside it, a standalone app called Cue. The account <a href="https://x.com/CueAgents/status/2104589146153181192" rel="noopener noreferrer">@CueAgents</a> introduced itself with a short line — personal agents, on Cue — and pointed at <a href="https://cue.im/" rel="noopener noreferrer">cue.im</a>. The paragraph that actually describes the product is section 3 of the <a href="https://manus.im/blog/introducing-manus-2-0" rel="noopener noreferrer">Manus 2.0 post</a>, “Cue: A new app from Manus.” This note is the day-after reading for people who already run DeepSeek Harness.</p>
  <p>Cue and DSH are not 1:1 substitutes. Cue is a closed consumer app for personal life operations. Each agent is issued an email, a phone number, a wallet, and a computer, and several of those agents can share a group chat. DSH is an MIT-licensed harness for people who build and customize agent runtimes: plugins, sandboxes, sessions, a Web UI you start yourself. Launch-day threads will rhyme them. This note keeps the categories apart.</p>

  <h2>What actually shipped</h2>
  <p>Cue is Manus’s personal multi-agent app, built on the same infrastructure as Manus, and offered on phone, desktop, and web. <a href="https://x.com/CueAgents/status/2104632868282769772" rel="noopener noreferrer">@CueAgents</a> described early access as free and limited, and named Android, Mac, and Windows as the clients you can open now. iOS has been submitted for App Store review, which is “coming soon,” not a build you can install from the store today. Early access is free with an invite code. A starter code, <strong>MEETCUE</strong>, has been mentioned in public as limited and first-come. Manus has not published a price for after early access. A missing price is a missing price. This note will not invent one.</p>
  <p>The feature that defines the product is the identity stack, which gets its own note in <a href="/blog/cue-agents-identity-stack-vs-dsh/">email, phone, wallet, computer</a>. Official copy says each agent can message, pay inside a budget you set, run tasks on its own machine, take calls, and leave the summary back in Cue. Phone numbers, Manus says, exist for Manus and Cue agents in select countries for now, and some of those lines are voice-only. There is no country list in this article, because publishing one would mean guessing.</p>
  <p>The multi-agent surface is a group chat with a shared goal. Agents hand work to each other. You set the direction and you keep the final call. The everyday example in the Manus post is the kind of chore a coding harness does not have a noun for: scan a restaurant QR code, and the agent orders or holds a place in line. That sentence is the category label. Cue is trying to act in the world on your behalf. DSH is trying to be the runtime under tools you write.</p>
  <p>Manus 2.0 also named Cascade, an in-house harness, plus Cloud Computer, Automations, and Manus Studio. That is context for the stack Cue sits on. Cascade’s percentages stay in <a href="/blog/manus-cascade-vs-deepseek-harness/">the Cascade note</a>, labeled as Manus’s own one-config self-test.</p>
  <p><a href="https://x.com/ManusAI/status/2104593894700990910" rel="noopener noreferrer">@ManusAI</a> framed the launch as introducing personal agents on Cue. Community summaries arrived within hours. <a href="https://x.com/bb833dong/status/2104714580081758274" rel="noopener noreferrer">@bb833dong</a> and <a href="https://x.com/ZachFeng/status/2104734255335219481" rel="noopener noreferrer">@ZachFeng</a> compressed the feature list for Chinese readers. <a href="https://x.com/FanShifu/status/2104714367162044817" rel="noopener noreferrer">@FanShifu</a> mused about use cases. Those posts are useful as a temperature reading. They are not a specification, and a tidy thread is not evidence that Cue and <code>dsh web</code> are two skins on one idea.</p>

