  <p>Read each row as a category difference. A cell that says DSH does not ship a phone number is a description of the job, not a bug filed against the harness. A cell that says Cue does not ship a forkable loop is a description of a consumer app, not a missing plugin.</p>

  <h2>Where they rhyme</h2>
  <p>Three ideas show up on both sides. They are why a comparison is worth writing, and why it has to stay inside those ideas.</p>
  <p><strong>Handoff.</strong> Cue’s group chat is a room where several agents share a goal and pass work. DSH’s team tab and shared task boards are a room where several sessions pass work. The <a href="/blog/wx-dsh-agent-team/">glass-room Agent Team article</a> already argued that a visible handoff is the point. The <a href="/blog/x-pulse-dsh-agent-teams-board/">board pulse</a> added the invoice: an unlimited handoff is a meeting that bills by the token. Cue makes the same bet inside a chat transcript. The practical map — approval, hop limits, when chat is enough — is in <a href="/blog/cue-group-chat-vs-dsh-agent-teams/">the group-chat guide</a>.</p>
  <p><strong>Each worker has a machine.</strong> Cue says each agent has a computer and can run tasks on it. DSH says a session can have a sandbox, tools, and a workspace. “Has a computer” is the rhyme. What that computer is allowed to touch — a restaurant line, a repository, a payment — is the divergence, and it is the whole subject of the identity note.</p>
  <p><strong>You remain the director.</strong> Cue’s copy keeps the final call with the person. DSH’s glass room keeps a project manager in the loop on purpose. Both products, on the evidence we have, still expect a human to set direction. A demo that implies a staff you cannot interrupt is ahead of either write-up.</p>

  <h2>Where they diverge</h2>
  <p><strong>Open plugins versus a closed app.</strong> DSH’s sentence is “everything is a plugin.” Models, tools, sandboxes, session storage, and UI are swappable modules on the Cordis kernel. You can read the loop. The repo is <a href="https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness" rel="noopener noreferrer">deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness</a>. The docs live at <a href="https://deepseek.com/harness/en/" rel="noopener noreferrer">deepseek.com/harness</a>. Cue does not offer that sentence. You receive an app. You do not receive a plugin store aimed at harness authors, and you do not receive a fork of the agent loop. That split is the same one this site already drew in <a href="/blog/vs-claude-code/">DeepSeek Harness vs Claude Code</a>, pointed this time at a life-ops product instead of a coding client.</p>
  <p><strong>Life operations versus a coding harness.</strong> Cue’s examples are messages, calls, payments inside a budget you set, and a restaurant QR. DSH’s examples are files, tools, traces, and plugins. A DSH reader who wants an agent to text a restaurant is looking at the wrong install command. A Cue reader who wants to swap the sandbox or pin a model is looking at the wrong app. The <a href="/blog/x-pulse-cue-agents-vs-dsh-chooser/">chooser</a> at the end of this batch is there so those two readers stop sharing one screenshot.</p>
  <p><strong>Bring your own model versus an opaque harness.</strong> DSH is explicit about the model. You point the runtime at a provider, the mode presets are yours to pick, and the preview nature of the APIs is part of the contract. Cue runs on Manus infrastructure. The new in-house harness under that stack is Cascade, and Manus has not published Cascade as open source. You can audit a DSH session. You cannot audit Cascade from the outside. Any efficiency percentage attached to Cascade stays a vendor self-test until someone who does not work at Manus can reproduce the configuration.</p>

  <h2>Who should open cue.im tonight</h2>
  <p>Open <a href="https://cue.im/" rel="noopener noreferrer">cue.im</a> if the thing you wanted this week was a personal agent with a real contact point: an inbox, a number, a budgeted wallet, a machine that is not your repository. The invite gate is part of the product. MEETCUE is a public starter code, and it is limited. If the code is exhausted, waiting is the state of early access. If you need iOS today, the App Store review is still in front of you. If you need a phone number, the official line is select countries, some of them voice-only, and a reply on X is not a coverage map.</p>
  <p>Stay on <code>npx @deepseek-ai/dsh web</code> if you are building. DSH is the lane where the loop, the tools, the sandbox, and the session are yours to replace. It is still a developer preview. Interfaces move. That is the cost of a harness you can read, and it is a different cost from a day-one consumer app. Use Cue when life-ops identity is the job. Use DSH when the job is the harness. The other four notes in this batch take one slice of that sentence each: the identity stack, the group chat, Cascade, and the launch-day chooser.</p>

